course: dictionary
lesson: 10642
title: Unicycle and the prefix uni-
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A unicycle is a word you can work out from its parts. Uni means one, and a cycle is a ride with pedals. So a unicycle is a cycle with only one wheel, a seat and pedals.
explain: «A unicycle has 1 wheel, a bicycle has 2 and a tricycle has 3.» Uni means one, bi means two, and tri means three. Once you know these prefixes, you can work out many other words.
explain: Maths problems often use these words. «4 bicycles, 2 tricycles and 3 unicycles» can be solved by multiplying each vehicle count by its wheels, then adding: 4 × 2 = 8, 2 × 3 = 6, 3 × 1 = 3, and 8 + 6 + 3 = 17 wheels.
fact: A unicycle is a cycle with only one wheel, pedals and a seat.
fact: You ride a unicycle by balancing, and clowns and performers often ride them.
fact: Uni means one, bi means two, and tri means three.
fact: To solve a counting problem, multiply the number of vehicles by their wheels, then add the answers.
fact: Unicycle can also be a verb, but it is rare: you will mostly meet it as a noun.
exercise: How many wheels does a unicycle have? | *1 | 2 | 4 | 3
exercise: In «4 bicycles, 2 tricycles and 3 unicycles», how many wheels are there in total? | 24 | 12 | *17 | 9
exercise: What does the prefix «uni» mean? | Three | *One | Two | Many
test: How many wheels does a tricycle have? | 2 | *3 | 1 | 4
test: How do you ride a unicycle? | By ringing a bell | By holding a rail | By pushing with your feet on the ground | *By balancing
exam: A maths problem has 5 unicycles and 2 bicycles. How many wheels are there in total? | 7 | 11 | 3 | *9
